  "appGroupStyleRule": {
    "id": "G1",
    "chosenStyle": "iOS-style",
    "statement": "Every App Group record this contract declares is allocated on the Developer website and claimed by the IDENTICAL literal on both platforms. The entitlement literal equals the portal record identifier exactly, on iOS and on macOS. No team prefix is added on macOS. How MANY records exist is not stated here: that count lives in appGroupDisjointnessRule.recordCount, which R11 checks against the record list, and in the two registered summary sentences R32 checks. r5 deleted the probe record and this sentence still said 'one record per family', which is the stale-summary class reviewer verdict 05 F1 named.",
    "rationale": "Apple distinguishes two App Group ID styles. An iOS-style ID begins 'group.', is allocated on the Developer website, and is authorised by a provisioning profile. A macOS-style ID begins with the Team ID and CANNOT be explicitly allocated on the Developer website. Since 21 Feb 2025 iOS-style App Group IDs are fully supported on macOS for all product types, and Xcode 16.3 and later make the iOS style the macOS default. This project is new and builds with Xcode 26.5, so it takes the current, registrable style on both platforms. A team-prefixed macOS literal would not identify the portal record this contract says it consumes, which is the defect this revision closes.",
    "source": "Apple DTS, https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/721701 — 'Starting in Feb 2025, iOS-style app group IDs are fully supported on macOS for all product types.' / 'On 21 Feb 2025 we rolled out a change to the Developer website that completes the support for iOS-style app group IDs on the Mac. Specifically, it's now possible to create a Mac provisioning profile that authorises the use of an iOS-style app group ID.' / 'With Xcode 16.3, it's now the default for macOS as well.' / on macOS-style IDs: 'It can't be explicitly allocated on the Developer website.' / 'If you're writing new code that uses app groups, use an iOS-style app group ID.'",
    "buildSetting": "Xcode's Register App Groups build setting governs this behaviour for existing projects; the generated targets are new, so the Xcode 16.3+ default applies. TASK-260715-uyju7n must not override it back to the macOS style.",
    "contingency": "OC-4"
  },
  "appGroupPurposeRule": {
    "id": "G2",
    "statement": "The App Group literal is identical across platforms (G1), but the entitlement is not granted on both. What the group is FOR exists on iOS and does not exist on macOS.",
    "iOS": "A genuinely shared filesystem container and UserDefaults suite. The host publishes the non-secret profile snapshot; the appex reads it. Host and appex run as the same user in the same context. Granted to the two PRODUCTION iOS targets, ios.host and ios.provider. Consumer: TASK-260715-3f4lxy.",
    "macOS": "No function, therefore no grant. The provider runs as root, so its group container resolves under /private/var/root while the host's resolves under the logged-in user's home; the two never meet. The macOS host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration for non-secret state plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and the XPC candidate that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name was rejected. com.apple.security.application-groups is prohibited on all four macOS targets under rule G4.",
    "prohibition": "No macOS design may depend on group-container files as a host-to-provider channel. TASK-260715-3f4lxy (profile snapshot loader) must read the macOS snapshot from providerConfiguration.",
    "evidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac: Tailscale's and Surfshark's packet-tunnel system extensions both ship com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/, which is direct evidence that a root provider's group container is not the user's. The full grant survey behind the macOS verdict is in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.",
    "iOSProbe": "Not granted. r5, on reviewer verdict 04 F3: TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the disposable probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container, so under G4 the probe has no App Group function of its own. Withholding it left the probe App Group record with no consumer, so the record is deleted too (rule G3) - a portal record no target reads is surface without a function."
  },
  "appGroupDisjointnessRule": {
    "id": "G3",
    "statement": "No probe target carries an App Group at all, on either platform. This is strictly stronger than the r4 property that the production and probe records were disjoint: with no probe grant there is no probe state that could reach production state, so the probe stays genuinely disposable by construction rather than by careful naming.",
    "recordCount": 1,
    "history": "r1-r4 declared a second record, group.works.relux.tunnel.probe, disjoint from production's and consumed by ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider. r5 withheld the entitlement from both under rule G4 (reviewer verdict 04 F3), which left that record with no consumer, so it is deleted rather than carried as deferred portal surface.",
    "ifReintroduced": "If a probe target ever regains an App Group under G4's reopening condition, its record MUST be a distinct iOS-style 'group.' literal disjoint from every production record, and this rule reverts to a disjointness test over two records."
  },
  "keychainAccessGroups": [
